# 🌳 DOM Manipulation Notes (Browser Console Edition)

You were working with:

```html
<h1 id="title" class="heading">DOM learning on chai aur code</h1>
```

---

## 1️⃣ `document.getElementById()`

```js
document.getElementById('title')
```

🔹 Returns the actual DOM element
🔹 Output:

```html
<h1 id="title" class="heading">...</h1>
```

Think of it as:

> "Yo browser, give me the element whose id is 'title'."

---

## 2️⃣ Accessing Properties

### ✅ `.id`

```js
document.getElementById('title').id
```

Output:

```
'title'
```

Because `.id` is a direct property of the element.

---

### ❌ `.class`

```js
document.getElementById('title').class
```

Output:

```
undefined
```

There is no `.class` property in DOM.

HTML uses `class`,
JavaScript uses **`className`**.

---

### ✅ `.className`

```js
document.getElementById('title').className
```

Output:

```
'heading'
```

Because JS uses `className` instead of `class`.

🧠 Why?
Because `class` is a reserved keyword in JavaScript (for ES6 classes).

---

## 3️⃣ `getAttribute()`

```js
document.getElementById('title').getAttribute('id')
```

Output:

```
'title'
```

🔹 Used to get ANY attribute from HTML.
🔹 Works even for custom attributes.

Difference:

| Property    | Attribute            |
| ----------- | -------------------- |
| `.id`       | `getAttribute('id')` |
| JS property | Raw HTML attribute   |

Most times they match — but not always.

---

## 4️⃣ `setAttribute()`

```js
document.getElementById('title').setAttribute('class', 'test')
```

⚠️ This REPLACED the existing class.

Before:

```
class="heading"
```

After:

```
class="test"
```

Then you did:

```js
setAttribute('class', 'test heading')
```

Now:

```
class="test heading"
```

So remember:

> `setAttribute()` overwrites. It does not append automatically.

If you want to add/remove classes properly → use `classList`.

Example:

```js
title.classList.add('newClass')
title.classList.remove('test')
```

---

## 5️⃣ Storing in a Variable

```js
const title = document.getElementById('title')
```

Now you don’t need to keep writing `document.getElementById()` again and again.

Clean code = happy brain.

---

## 6️⃣ Styling via `.style`

You did:

```js
title.style.backgroundColor = 'green'
title.style.padding = "15px"
```

This directly modifies inline CSS.

Important rule:

CSS → kebab-case
JS → camelCase

| CSS              | JavaScript      |
| ---------------- | --------------- |
| background-color | backgroundColor |
| border-radius    | borderRadius    |

That’s why:

```js
title.style.borderradius = "30px"
```

❌ didn’t work

But:

```js
title.style.borderRadius = "30px"
```

✅ worked

---

## ⚡ Why Did Console Show `"green"` and `"15px"`?

Because when you assign a value, JS returns the assigned value.

```js
title.style.padding = "15px"
```

Returns:

```
'15px'
```

It’s just the assignment expression returning the value.

