innerHTML vs innerText vs textContent
======================================

You had something like:

```html
<h1 id="title">
  DOM learning on chai aur code 
  <span style="display : none;">test text</span>
</h1>
```

Now look at what each property does 👇

---

# 🧠 1️⃣ `innerText`

```js
title.innerText
```

Output:

```
'DOM learning on chai aur code'
```

### ✅ What it does:

* Returns **only visible text**
* Respects CSS
* Ignores `display: none`
* Triggers layout recalculation (slightly heavier)

Since your `<span>` had:

```html
style="display : none;"
```

That text is hidden → so `innerText` ignored it.

💡 Think of `innerText` as:

> “What would the USER actually see on screen?”

---

# 🧠 2️⃣ `textContent`

```js
title.textContent
```

Output:

```
'DOM learning on chai aur code test text'
```

### ✅ What it does:

* Returns **all text**
* Ignores styling
* Includes hidden text
* Faster than `innerText`

It doesn’t care that your span is hidden.
It just reads raw text from the DOM tree.

💡 Think of `textContent` as:

> “Give me ALL the text nodes inside this element. I don’t care about styling.”

---

# 🧠 3️⃣ `innerHTML`

```js
title.innerHTML
```

Output:

```
'DOM learning on chai aur code <span style="display : none;">test text</span>'
```

### ✅ What it does:

* Returns full HTML inside the element
* Includes tags
* Includes inline styles

It doesn’t just give text — it gives the entire markup.

💡 Think of `innerHTML` as:

> “Give me everything inside this element exactly as written in HTML.”

---

# 🔥 Clean Comparison Table

| Property    | Includes Hidden Text? | Includes HTML Tags? | Respects CSS? |
| ----------- | --------------------- | ------------------- | ------------- |
| innerText   | ❌ No                  | ❌ No                | ✅ Yes         |
| textContent | ✅ Yes                 | ❌ No                | ❌ No          |
| innerHTML   | ✅ Yes                 | ✅ Yes               | ❌ No          |

---

# 🚀 When To Use What?

### Use `innerText`

When you care about what the user sees.

Example:

* Getting visible content for display logic.

---

### Use `textContent`

When you want raw text (usually preferred).

Example:

* Reading content
* Updating text safely
* Better performance

---

### Use `innerHTML`

When you want to:

* Inject HTML
* Add new tags dynamically

Example:

```js
title.innerHTML = "<b>Hello</b>"
```

⚠️ But be careful:
Using `innerHTML` with user input can cause XSS security issues.

---

# ⚡ Interview Gold Tip

If they ask:

> What’s the difference between innerText and textContent?

You say:

* `innerText` respects CSS and returns only visible text.
* `textContent` returns all text, including hidden elements.
* `textContent` is faster.

Boom. 2 marks secured.

