# 🔎 `document.querySelector()`

It selects the **FIRST element** that matches a CSS selector.

Syntax:

```js
document.querySelector("css-selector")
```

---

## 1️⃣ Selecting by tag

```js
document.querySelector('h2')
```

✔ Selects the **first `<h2>`** on the page.

Even if there are 10 `<h2>` tags, it will only return the first one.

---

## 2️⃣ Selecting by ID

```js
document.querySelector('#title')
```

Notice the `#`.

In CSS:

* `#` → ID
* `.` → Class

This is exactly like writing CSS:

```css
#title { }
```

It returns:

```html
<h1 id="title" class="heading">...</h1>
```

So technically:

```js
document.querySelector('#title')
```

and

```js
document.getElementById('title')
```

Both give the same result.

But `querySelector` is more powerful because it can do way more than just ID.

---

## 3️⃣ Selecting by class

```js
document.querySelector('.heading')
```

Returns the first element with class `"heading"`.

Even if 20 elements have that class, it will only give the first one.

---

# ⚡ Difference: `getElementById` vs `querySelector`

| Method         | Accepts             | Returns                |
| -------------- | ------------------- | ---------------------- |
| getElementById | Only ID (without #) | Single element         |
| querySelector  | ANY CSS selector    | First matching element |

So if you're building modern apps → `querySelector` is more flexible.

---

# 💎 Where `querySelector` Becomes Powerful

You can do things like:

### Nested selection

```js
document.querySelector('#title span')
```

👉 Selects span inside #title

---

### Multiple classes

```js
document.querySelector('.heading.test')
```

👉 Element that has BOTH classes

---

### Attribute selectors

```js
document.querySelector('span[style]')
```

👉 Selects span that has style attribute

---

# 🚀 Bonus: Want ALL matches?

Use:

```js
document.querySelectorAll('.heading')
```

This returns a **NodeList** (like a mini array).

You can loop over it.

---

# 🧠 When to Use What in Real Projects?

If:

* You only need ID → `getElementById()` (slightly faster)
* You need flexibility → `querySelector()`
* You need multiple elements → `querySelectorAll()`

In React world?
You rarely use these directly because React handles DOM for you.
But knowing this makes you understand how React works under the hood.

